    "https://www.livehire.com/careers/universityofnewcastle/job/CD84Q/5KFEMO425K/data-specialist-neuroimaging-analytics-and-infrastructure-brain-scan-program": {
      "title": "Data Specialist Neuroimaging Analytics and Infrastructure (BRAIN-SCAN Program)",
      "description": "Data Specialist Neuroimaging Analytics and Infrastructure (BRAIN-SCAN Program) - University of Newcastle | Humanforce Talent Log in Humanforce LinkedIn Facebook Google Cancel Powered by ‹ Back to all jobs Data Specialist Neuroimaging Analytics and Infrastructure (BRAIN-SCAN Program) Location New Lambton Heights NSW 2305, Australia Work Type Full Time - Fixed Term Positions 1 Position Published At: 4 days ago Loading Refer someone Apply Job no: HRC0026472 Category: Professional, HEW 7 Join the BRAIN-SCAN team building national capability for neuroimaging data analysis in brain cancer Opportunity to work alongside visionary leaders committed to improving patient outcomes 17% super, generous annual, personal, and life leave, health & fitness discounts Be challenged and imagine with us Join a pioneering team at the forefront of brain cancer imaging research and help shape the future of neuroimaging in Australia. As a Data Specialist Neuroimaging Analytics and Infrastructure within the cutting-edge BRAIN-SCAN initiative, you will develop and provide advanced imaging analytics that support researchers, clinicians and PhD candidates throughout their research journey. This role is a joint effort between the Mark Hughes Foundation Centre for Brain Cancer Research (MHF Centre) at the University of Newcastle (UoN) and the Brain Cancer Centre (BCC) at the Walter and Eliza Hall Institute for Medical Research (WEHI). Working alongside visionary clinical and research leaders in neuro-oncology, medical imaging, neuroscience and data science, you will drive innovative approaches to analysing complex datasets, contribute to life-changing discoveries, and play a meaningful role in improving outcomes for people affected by brain cancer. This is a rare opportunity to join a one-of-a-kind team that is transforming how neuroimaging data is used to accelerate research and collaboration across Australia. In this role you will: Lead the analysis and interpretation of large-scale neuroimaging datasets. Deliver high-quality analytical outputs that inform research priorities and decision-making. Develop and optimise neuroimaging analysis pipelines, workflows and methodologies. Collaborate with researchers, clinicians and industry partners to advance brain cancer research including National Imaging Facility (NIF) sites across Australia. Support graduate researchers and PhD candidates with imaging data analysis and interpretation. Translate complex datasets into meaningful insights, reports and recommendations. Contribute to the development of Australia's national neuroimaging infrastructure. Ensure robust data quality, governance and reproducibility standards. Identify opportunities to innovate and enhance analytical approaches and research outcomes. Play a key role in delivering outcomes for the BRAIN-SCAN initiative. Have the rare opportunity to work with a pioneering team that is unique in Australia. This full-time, 1 Year – Fixed Term, is at a HEW 7, and located at the Hunter Medical Research Institute Building (HMRI) (Awabakal and Worimi land). How will you inspire us? You will: Bring expertise in neuroimaging, medical imaging, data analytics or a related field. Be passionate about using data to advance research and improve outcomes for people with brain cancer. Demonstrate strong analytical and problem-solving skills when working with complex datasets. Lead and influence through technical expertise and collaboration. Translate data into clear insights that support evidence-based decision-making. Build effective relationships with researchers, clinicians, students and research partners. Thrive in a multidisciplinary environment focused on innovation and discovery. Take initiative and drive continuous improvement in analytical methods and processes. Communicate complex technical concepts clearly to diverse audiences. About our University The University of Newcastle stands as a global leader distinguished by a commitment to equity and excellence. Our vision is to be a world-leading University for our regions and create a better future for our regions and the globe. Underpinned by this vision are our values of excellence, equity, engagement and sustainability. About the Unit Created in 2022, the MHF Centre for Brain Cancer Research, a partnership between the University of Newcastle and the Mark Hughes Foundation (MHF), is a multi-disciplinary, collaborative research centre which brings together scientists and clinicians, consumers and patient care professionals and builds on the University of Newcastle’s established and dynamic cancer research ecosystem and world-class technology, spaces, and infrastructure. Through collaboration and partnership with our community and like-minded national and international organisations, the Centre will drive world-leading research programs to improve the lives of people with brain cancer today and increase survival rates in the future. What we offer In addition to working for a university that is breaking boundaries, discovering new ways of thinking and cementing a place as a global leader, we offer a range of benefits to our employees to ensure you balance your commitments, stay healthy and work effectively. These include: Remuneration of Higher Education Worker (HEW) Level 7 ($107,268-$120,677) + 17% superannuation, commensurate with skills and experience. Additional University Holiday days, generous personal, life, and parental leave, annual leave loading, and additional purchased leave scheme. Discounts on private health insurance, gym memberships, software, travel and more. A range of support and wellbeing programs. For more information on benefits and conditions, please follow this link . Your next steps Important information before you apply: If viewing this on a job board other than the University's please click ‘Apply’ (or equivalent) to reach the University's website, where a copy of the Position Description can be accessed. Please download a copy from the Related Documents section before commencing your application. Applications for this position will only be accepted from those with Australian residency or a valid work permit. Your application must include: Your resume. A cover letter outlining your motivation to apply for this opportunity. A statement addressing each of the essential criteria listed below. Essential Criteria A degree in neuroscience, biomedical engineering, medical imaging, data science or a related discipline with at least 4 years subsequent relevant experience; or an equivalent combination of relevant qualifications and research experience. Demonstrated experience working with neuroimaging data formats such as DICOM and NIfTI. Demonstrated experience developing computational pipelines or analytical workflows for imaging data. Programming experience in languages such as Python, Matlab or R and experience working in Linux-based computing environments. Please upload your Cover Letter and response to the requested Criteria as one PDF document . This document should not exceed 3 pages. We value diversity and strive to ensure that our recruitment processes are equitable, inclusive, and barrier-free. If you require an alternative pathway to view the Position Description or to submit an application, please reach out to employment@newcastle.edu.au . We also seek to provide a fair chance to all applicants by providing reasonable adjustments where required during the assessment and interview phases of the recruitment process. Our assessment process Typically, candidates that progress beyond the shortlist stage can expect to undertake the following (minimum) assessment activities as part of the recruitment process: Interview (an ‘in person’ interview will be conducted as part of the process) Complete a task prior to the in person interview Referee checks Pre-employment checks as appropriate to the role Our University is committed to creating a safe, respectful, and inclusive environment for every member of our community . This commitment is central to our values and our efforts to build a diverse workforce where everyone can thrive. As part of this, and in alignment with the National Higher Health Sciences Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-Based Violence 2025 , from 1 January 2026, the University will also consider gender-based violence risk factors as part of our recruitment processes. The completion and return of the University’s Gender-based Violence Declaration Form will form part of the pre-employment checks for this position and any offer of employment will be conditional upon review by the University. Our adherence to the Code is about more than compliance – it’s about living our values. Together, we can ensure our University remains a place where respect, safety, and inclusion are non-negotiable. Contact Us For specific position enquiries, please contact Bryan Paton, Research Associate, on email at: bryan.paton@newcastle.edu.au Having technical difficulties? Please reach out to us: employment@newcastle.edu.au Please note, we do not accept email applications. Closing date: Monday, 26 October 2026 at 11.59pm AEST We are excited to be Looking Ahead with you! We provide opportunities for all people regardless of their background and experience, and this philosophy is reflected across all that we do. The University of Newcastle is an equal opportunity employer committed to equity, diversity, and social inclusion. Women and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander candidates are particularly encouraged to apply. #LI-CJ1 Related documents PD - Data Specialist Neuroimaging Analytics and Infrastructure Published on 09 Oct 2026, 1:37 AM Back to top Refer someone Powered by Privacy Policy Terms of Service"
    },
    "https://www.livehire.com/careers/universityofnewcastle/job/QAAYE/4SYDU8954K/hdr-program-enhancement-coordinator-activations": {
      "title": "HDR Program Enhancement Coordinator (Activations)",
      "description": "HDR Program Enhancement Coordinator (Activations) - University of Newcastle | Humanforce Talent Log in Humanforce LinkedIn Facebook Google Cancel Powered by ‹ Back to all jobs HDR Program Enhancement Coordinator (Activations) Location Callaghan NSW 2308, Australia Work Type Full Time - Fixed Term Positions 1 Position Published At: 5 days ago Loading Refer someone Apply Job no: HRC0027842 Category: Professional, HEW 7 Drive innovative initiatives shaping the future of research education. Lead the coordination of advice and support related to HDR programs and services. 17% super, generous annual, personal, and life leave, health & fitness discounts. The Role As the HDR Program Enhancement Coordinator (Activations), you will lead a diverse portfolio of projects that support continuous improvement within HDR programs and the broader research student experience. Working closely with internal and external stakeholders, you will deliver strategic initiatives from concept through to implementation and evaluation, applying expertise in project management, quality assurance and stakeholder engagement to ensure alignment with regulatory requirements and best practice. In this role you will: Lead HDR enhancement and activation projects that support quality, compliance and the research student experience. Manage project delivery, including planning, stakeholder engagement, implementation and evaluation. Coordinate key HDR initiatives and events, including conferences, festivals, assessment integrity projects and the implementation of HDR quality assurance frameworks. Provide expert advice and support to Schools, Colleges and Divisions on HDR quality assurance, compliance and program improvement. Use data, feedback and sector insights to drive continuous improvement and strengthen HDR programs. Build collaborative relationships across the University and externally to achieve project outcomes, benchmark practices and share best practice. This 18-month Fixed-term position is full time and will be located at the Callaghan Campus (Awabakal and Worimi land). How will you inspire us? You will have: Demonstrated knowledge of higher education research governance frameworks and experience in implementing quality assurance processes to ensure compliance with national standards and best practice in PhD supervision and research environments. Excellent communication, interpersonal and negotiation skills, including the ability to manage difficult conversations, as well as effectively engage with a culturally diverse range of internal and external stakeholders. Excellent analytical, problem-solving and organisational skills, including managing multiple priorities with exceptional attention to detail. Demonstrated capacity to work within a team and bring a strong, positive culture to achieve divisional and organisational goals in an environment of competing demands. About our University The University of Newcastle stands as a global leader distinguished by a commitment to equity and excellence. Our vision is to be a world-leading University for our regions and creating a better future for our regions and the globe. Underpinned by this vision are our values of excellence, equity, engagement and sustainability. About the Unit Graduate Research is responsible for building, shaping and implementing initiatives to develop the next generation of research leaders at the University of Newcastle; and for the timely and efficient management of Higher Degree Research (HDR) activities within the University, including the administration of HDR rules and policies; compliance with relevant university and external regulatory requirements; and maintaining the highest ethical standards in research higher degree training. The team is responsible for the above services for both domestic and international students. What we offer In addition to working for a university that is breaking boundaries, discovering new ways of thinking and cementing a place as a global leader, we offer a range of benefits to our employees to ensure you balance your commitments, stay healthy and work effectively. These include: Remuneration of Higher Education Worker (HEW) Level 7 ($107,268 - $120,677) + 17% superannuation, commensurate with skills and experience. Additional University Holiday days, generous personal, life, and parental leave, annual leave loading, and additional purchased leave scheme. Discounts on private health insurance, gym memberships, software, travel and more. A range of support and wellbeing programs. For more information on benefits and conditions, please follow this link . Your next steps Important information before you apply: If viewing this on a job board other than the University's please click ‘Apply’ (or equivalent) to reach the University's website, where a copy of the Position Description can be accessed. Please download a copy from the Related Documents section before commencing your application. Applications for this position will only be accepted from those with Australian residency or a valid work permit. Your application must include: Your resume. A cover letter outlining your motivation to apply for this opportunity. A statement addressing each of the 4 essential criteria listed below the ‘ How will you inspire us – you will have:’ heading in this advert using an example for each to demonstrate (these may differ slightly from the Position Description). Please upload your Cover Letter and response to the requested Criteria as one PDF document . This document should not exceed 3 pages. We value diversity and strive to ensure that our recruitment processes are equitable, inclusive, and barrier-free. If you require an alternative pathway to view the Position Description or to submit an application, please reach out to employment@newcastle.edu.au . We also seek to provide a fair chance to all applicants by providing reasonable adjustments where required during the assessment and interview phases of the recruitment process. Our assessment process Typically candidates that progress beyond the shortlist stage can expect to undertake the following (minimum) assessment activities as part of the recruitment process: Interview (an ‘in person’ interview will be conducted as part of the process) Referee checks Pre-employment checks as appropriate to the role Our University is committed to creating a safe, respectful, and inclusive environment for every member of our community . This commitment is central to our values and our efforts to build a diverse workforce where everyone can thrive. As part of this, and in alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-Based Violence 2025 , from 1 January 2026, the University will also consider gender-based violence risk factors as part of our recruitment processes. The completion and return of the University’s Gender-based Violence Declaration Form will form part of the pre-employment checks for this position and any offer of employment will be conditional upon review by the University. Our adherence to the Code is about more than compliance – it’s about living our values. Together, we can ensure our University remains a place where respect, safety, and inclusion are non-negotiable. Contact Us For specific position enquiries, please contact Gareth Patterson, Manager Governance and Development via email Gareth.Patterson@newcastle.edu.au Having technical difficulties? Please reach out to us: employment@newcastle.edu.au Please note, we do not accept email applications. Closing date: Monday, 26 October 2026 at 11.59pm AEDT We are excited to be Looking Ahead with you We provide opportunities for all people regardless of their background and experience, and this philosophy is reflected across all that we do. The University of Newcastle is an equal opportunity employer committed to equity, diversity, and social inclusion. Women and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander candidates are particularly encouraged to apply. #LI-CJ1 Related documents PD - HDR Program Enhancement Coordinator (Activations) Published on 08 Oct 2026, 4:33 AM Back to top Refer someone Powered by Privacy Policy Terms of Service"
