    "https://www.livehire.com/careers/universityofnewcastle/job/4YHE7/5MJNJ95USG/chief-operating-officer": {
      "title": "Chief Operating Officer",
      "description": "Chief Operating Officer - University of Newcastle | Humanforce Talent Opens in a new window Opens an external website Opens an external website in a new window Close this consent banner This website utilizes technologies such as cookies to enable essential site functionality, as well as for analytics, personalization, and targeted advertising. To learn more, view the following link: Privacy Policy Storage Preferences Log in Humanforce LinkedIn Facebook Google Cancel Powered by ‹ Back to all jobs Chief Operating Officer Location Callaghan NSW 2308, Australia Work Type Full Time - Fixed Term Positions 1 Position Applications Close At Closes in 21 days Published on 13 Oct 2026 Apply I'm interested Loading Powered by Humanforce Talent Refer someone Join our Talent Community Not the job you're looking for? Become a member of our talent community to stay connected and be informed of any new opportunities. Sign up Already a member? Log in Job no: COO260924 Category: Professional The University of Newcastle stands as a global top 1% University, committed to delivering an exceptional student experience, preparing graduates for life, and with a shared purpose to better our regions and the world. Our Looking Ahead 2030 Strategic Plan captures the spirit of looking to the future to imagine and then realise possibilities, enabled by the values of excellence, equity, engagement and sustainability. Reporting directly to the Vice-Chancellor, and a member of the senior executive group, the University of Newcastle is seeking their next Chief Operating Officer (COO). The role is responsible for overseeing the resources, operations and infrastructure of the University and its campuses and for creating an operating environment to enable the achievement of the University’s strategic priorities for reimagining campuses, inspiring people, providing an outstanding student experience and serving the community. The Resources Division (COO portfolio) includes Strategy, Planning and Performance, Financial Services, Infrastructure and Facilities Services, Human Resource Services, Digital Technology Services, Risk Services and the University Library. The COO has a broad remit, and it will be essential to mobilise a large professional team to deliver upon the University’s Strategic Plan (Looking Ahead 2030). The COO will also represent the University on relevant external boards and committees and play an advocacy role for the University’s operational activities and initiatives within the broader community. We are seeking an experienced senior executive with gravitas and an inclusive leadership style, relevant qualifications, and extensive experience in a similar leadership role within a large, complex, values-led organisation. Strong resource management and experience leading a similar portfolio should be complemented by excellent communication skills, sound judgement and an ability to build strong relationships with the University Council, Executive Leadership Team, senior management and stakeholders across and beyond the University. The COO will embed a culture of innovation, collaboration, service and improved ways of working which optimises the growth, achievements and sustainability of the University. For a confidential discussion about this outstanding position and to request the candidate information booklet, please contact Janine Fitzgerald via +61 424 164 765 or jfitzgerald@robinsonfitzgerald.com.au , or Kate Robinson via +61 409 769 560 or krobinson@robinsonfitzgerald.com.au quoting reference RF0275 . Applications can be sent to either of the above email addresses and will close on the 6 th of November 2026. The University of Newcastle is an equal opportunity employer committed to equity, diversity and social inclusion. Women and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander candidates are particularly encouraged to apply. The University of Newcastle acknowledges the traditional custodians of the lands within our footprint areas: Awabakal, Darkinjung, Birpai, Worimi, Wonnarua, and Eora Nations. We also pay respect to the wisdom of our Elders past and present. Closes in 21 days - 06 Nov 2026 Published on 13 Oct 2026, 11:47 PM Back to top Refer someone Join our Talent Community Not the job you're looking for? Become a member of our talent community to stay connected and be informed of any new opportunities. Sign up Already a member? Log in Powered by Privacy Policy Terms of Service Cookie Preference s"
    },
    "https://www.livehire.com/careers/universityofnewcastle/job/EAMHD/MGBEJZ5ATW/campus-care-coordinator": {
      "title": "Campus Care Coordinator",
      "description": "Campus Care Coordinator - University of Newcastle | Humanforce Talent Log in Humanforce LinkedIn Facebook Google Cancel Powered by ‹ Back to all jobs Campus Care Coordinator Location Callaghan NSW 2308, Australia Work Type Full Time - Fixed Term Positions 1 Position Published At: 4 days ago Loading Refer someone Apply Job no: HRC0027564 Category: Professional, HEW 8 Join a highly skilled clinical team in a behavioural and threat management role Influence policy and continuous improvement Full time Fixed Term Role until August 2027 Based at Callaghan Be Challenged and Imagine with us Do you have experience assessing and managing high-risk or complex behavioural situations? Are you calm under pressure, highly empathetic, and skilled at balancing risk with compassionate support? The University of Newcastle is seeking a Campus Care Coordinator to join our behavioural threat and risk management team (Campus Care), where you'll play a critical role in helping create a safe, respectful and supportive environment for the University Community. Working at the intersection of risk management, wellbeing and case coordination, you'll respond to reports of inappropriate, concerning or threatening behaviour, including sexual assault and sexual harassment. Through evidence-based behavioural risk assessment, collaborative case management and strong stakeholder relationships, you'll help identify concerns early, coordinate appropriate responses, and minimise harm. No two days are the same. This is a varied and rewarding role where you'll work closely with internal and external stakeholders, providing expert advice and coordinating responses to complex situations that require sound judgement, discretion and professionalism. The Role In this role you will: Receive, assess and respond to reports of concerning, inappropriate or threatening behaviour through the University's Campus Care framework. Conduct behavioural threat and risk assessments using evidence-based tools. Develop tailored risk management, safety and referral plans to support individuals and manage potential harm. Coordinate responses to high-risk incidents, ensuring timely and appropriate action is taken. Work collaboratively with internal services, external agencies to support effective case management. Provide advice and guidance to staff and students while maintaining privacy, confidentiality and procedural fairness. Contribute to the ongoing promotion of Campus Care and initiatives that strengthen a safe and respectful University community. Drive continuous improvement in service delivery and behavioural risk management practices. Will exercise highly developed written communication skills, including experience preparing professional reports, case notes, correspondence, briefings and recommendations. Essential Criteria: A tertiary qualification in Social Work, Psychology, Counselling, Occupational Therapy, Nursing, or another relevant discipline, with subsequent relevant experience; and registration with nationally recognised professional board or bodies (e.g. AHPRA, AASW). Demonstrated experience working with individuals experiencing complex wellbeing, mental health, behavioural or safety concerns within a health, community services, higher education, justice or similar setting. Experience applying professional judgement and evidenced based tools to assess and manage behavioural threat and risk, develop risk management strategies, and support decision-making in complex or sensitive situations. Outstanding interpersonal, communication and relationship-building skills, with the ability to engage confidently with a broad range of stakeholders, and communicate effectively with individuals experiencing distress, conflict or crisis. Demonstrated experience responding to individuals impacted by trauma, including the ability to apply trauma-informed, person-centred and culturally safe approaches. Knowledge of current practice in responding to gender-based violence, family and domestic violence, sexual harm and other forms of interpersonal violence, including victim-survivor centred approaches. This fixed term role is full time at a HEW 8 Higher Education Worker Level until August 2027 and is located at Callaghan Campus. About our University The University of Newcastle stands as a global leader distinguished by a commitment to equity and excellence. Our vision is to be a world-leading University for our regions and creating a better future for our regions and the globe. Underpinned by this vision and our values of excellence, equity, engagement and sustainability, the University of Newcastle’s Looking Ahead Strategic Plan 20 30 outlines the University’s commitment to delivering an exceptional student experience and serving our communities. For more information click here – Our University. What we offer In addition to working for a university that is breaking boundaries, discovering new ways of thinking and cementing a place as a global leader, we offer a range of benefits to our employees to ensure you balance your commitments, stay healthy and work effectively. These include: A remuneration of $120677 - $140791p.a + superannuation commensurate with skills and experience Excellent employment conditions in line with University of Newcastle professional staff conditions. Flexible working arrangements (in line with the University’s Flexible Work Policy 35 hour week 17% super and salary packaging Additional University Holiday days, generous personal leave and parental leave, annual leave loading and additional purchased leave scheme Discounts in private health insurance, gym memberships, software, travel and more Wellbeing programs Your next steps Please visit here for a guide on how to apply for a role. If viewing this on a job board other than the University's please click ‘Apply’ to reach the University's website, where a copy of the Position Description can be accessed. Please download a copy before commencing your application. Your application must include: Your resume A cover letter outlining your motivation to apply for this opportunity A statement addressing the Essential Criteria listed in the job description above demonstrating your qualifications and experience with examples. To ensure that your application receives full consideration, it's important to structure and address the selection criteria outlined in the application process. This helps us better understand your experience and suitability for the role. We appreciate your attention to this matter and look forward to reviewing your application. Please upload your Cover Letter and response to the requested Criteria as one PDF document. Your Cover Letter and Response to the Essential Criteria should not exceed 2 pages (approximately 1,000 words). Applications for this position will only be accepted from those with Australian residency or a valid work permit. We are unable to offer visa sponsorship for this role. For specific position enquiries, please contact Scott Laing, Associate Director, Student Wellbeing (02) 4055 0672 or via email Scott.Laing@newcastle.edu.au Having Difficulties? Please reach out to us: employment@newcastle.edu.au Closing date: Sunday 25 th October 2026 @ 11:59pm AEST Our assessment process Typically, candidates that progress beyond the shortlist stage can expect to undertake the following (minimum) assessment activities as part of the recruitment process: Interview (an ‘in person’ interview will be conducted as part of the process) Referee checks National Police and Working With Children checks as appropriate to the role Our University is committed to creating a safe, respectful, and inclusive environment for every member of our community. This commitment is central to our values and our efforts to build a diverse workforce where everyone can thrive. As part of this, and in alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-Based Violence 2025 , from 1 January 2026, the University will also consider gender-based violence risk factors as part of our recruitment processes. The completion and return of the University’s Gender-based Violence Declaration Form will form part of the pre-employment checks for this position and any offer of employment will be conditional upon review by the University. Our adherence to the Code is about more than compliance—it’s about living our values. Together, we can ensure our University remains a place where respect, safety, and inclusion are non-negotiable. We are excited to be Looking Ahead with you We are committed to a fair, inclusive, and barrier-free recruitment process. If you require an alternative way to access the Position Description or submit your application, please contact employment@newcastle.edu.au . Reasonable adjustments are available throughout the assessment and interview process to support all applicants to participate fully. The University of Newcastle is an equal opportunity employer committed to equity, diversity, and social inclusion. We welcome applications from people of all backgrounds and strongly encourage women and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander candidates to apply. #LI-KG1 Related documents PD Campus Care Coordinator New (1) Published on 13 Oct 2026, 4:30 AM Back to top Refer someone Powered by Privacy Policy Terms of Service"
    },
    "https://www.livehire.com/careers/universityofnewcastle/job/M73BR/0CTQT3X5UJ/lecturer-computing-and-information-technology-teaching-focused": {
      "title": "Lecturer Computing and Information Technology (Teaching Focused)",
      "description": "Lecturer Computing and Information Technology (Teaching Focused) - University of Newcastle | Humanforce Talent Log in Humanforce LinkedIn Facebook Google Cancel Powered by ‹ Back to all jobs Lecturer Computing and Information Technology (Teaching Focused) Location Callaghan NSW 2308, Australia Work Type Full Time - Fixed Term Positions 1 Position Published At: 4 days ago Loading Refer someone Apply Job no: HRC0026967 Category: Academic, Level B Inspire future technology professionals through innovative teaching Deliver engaging, industry-relevant Computing and IT education Shape curriculum and help students build future-ready careers Category: Academic Level B Salary: $121,329 to $144,079 plus 17% superannuation plus annual leave loading Position: Full-time, 5-year fixed-term Location: Callaghan Campus About the Role As a Lecturer in Computing and Information Technology (Teaching Focused), you will play a key role in delivering high-quality teaching and creating engaging learning experiences that support student success. You will contribute to curriculum development, course coordination, and continuous improvement across undergraduate and postgraduate programs, while embracing contemporary and technology-enhanced teaching practices. Working collaboratively with colleagues, you will support the School's academic and strategic priorities, foster industry connections that enhance student learning, and supervise student projects and research candidates. You will also engage in scholarly and research activities that maintain disciplinary expertise and inform teaching practice. About You You are dedicated and forward-thinking, with expertise in Computing and Information Technology and a passion for delivering outstanding student learning experiences. You bring a strong foundation in areas such as artificial intelligence, cybersecurity, quantum computing, or related disciplines, alongside a commitment to innovative teaching, scholarly activity, and industry engagement. To be successful in this role, you will have: A PhD in Computer Science, Software Engineering, IT or a related area, or equivalent relevant field. Ability to be a highly effective teacher in a tertiary environment, preferably with experience in teaching and curriculum development in Computing and IT related courses at the University level. Demonstrated ability to supervise research students at both undergraduate and postgraduate levels. Demonstrated ability to conduct impactful research to attract industry/government research funding. Expertise or experience in the development and delivery of online and blended courses. For the full list of essential and desirable selection criteria, please review the Position Description. If you’re ready to join a university that’s committed to equity and excellence, and to contribute to a community that values your expertise and vision, we encourage you to apply. About Us The School of Computer and Information Sciences is a dynamic centre for innovative teaching and globally recognised research. As part of the College of Engineering, Science and Environment, we deliver future-focused programs in Computer Science, Cyber Security, Data Science, Information Technology, Mathematics, Software Engineering, and Statistics. Our graduates develop the analytical and technical capabilities needed to succeed in rapidly evolving industries, while our researchers contribute to advances in areas such as applied mathematics, machine learning, and space sciences. Through strong industry partnerships, interdisciplinary collaboration, and a supportive learning environment, we are committed to empowering students and driving innovation that delivers real-world impact. Our University The University of Newcastle stands as a global leader with a reputation for excellence in education and research. Ranking in the top 1% of global universities and among the top 40 for impact, our vision is to be a world-leading University for our regions and create a better future for our regions and the globe. Underpinned by this vision and our values of excellence, equity, engagement and sustainability, outlines the University’s commitment to delivering an exceptional student experience and serving our communities. Our Strategic Plan Looking Ahead 2030 outlines the University’s commitment to delivering an exceptional student experience and serving our communities. We are proud to be one of the first two Universities in Australia to achieve SAGE Athena Swan Silver Accreditation for gender equity, diversity, and inclusion (GEDI). We acknowledge the traditional custodians of the lands within our footprint areas and extend respect to Elders past and present. We are committed to strengthening Indigenous higher education and supporting Indigenous students and communities. For more information click here . What We Offer The university offers a comprehensive and inclusive benefits package designed to support employee wellbeing, career development, and work-life balance for all staff, including: Flexible working arrangements 17% superannuation, access to salary packaging options and annual leave loading Generous leave entitlements Wellbeing and support programs that promote mental, physical, and emotional health Career development opportunities Great Lifestyle – Enjoy the best of coastal living in Newcastle, with stunning natural beauty, a thriving cultural scene, and easy connectivity to Sydney and other major cities. Visit the City of Newcastle site to imagine your future in Newcastle. For further information on our benefits and perks, please visit our website . How to Apply If viewing this on a job board other than the University's please click ‘Apply’ to be redirected to our website, where you can review the full Position Description and submit your application. Your application must include: Your ORCID ID. A URL link to your University Academic Profile (or equivalent). A Cover Letter and Statement (uploaded as a single PDF): Cover Letter (max. 1 page) outlining your interest in the role and providing details of 3 referees. Statement (max. 4 pages) addressing the Essential and Desirable Criteria, as listed in the position description. Your CV (max. 5 pages) tailored to your discipline Please visit here for a guide on how to apply for a role. It’s important you read this information to ensure you are providing all the requirements of your application. For specific position enquiries, please contact Associate Professor Elena Levchenko, Acting Head of School, School of Computer and Information Sciences at CESE-SCIS-HOS@newcastle.edu.au Our Assessment Process Typically candidates that progress beyond the shortlist stage can expect to undertake the following (minimum) assessment activities as part of the recruitment process: Interview (an ‘in person’ interview will be conducted as part of the process) Referee checks Pre-employment checks as appropriate to the role Our University is committed to creating a safe, respectful, and inclusive environment for every member of our community. This commitment is central to our values and our efforts to build a diverse workforce where everyone can thrive. As part of this, and in alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-Based Violence 2025 , from 1 January 2026, the University will also consider gender-based violence risk factors as part of our recruitment processes. The completion and return of the University’s Gender-based Violence Declaration Form will form part of the pre-employment checks for this position and any offer of employment will be conditional upon review by the University. Our adherence to the Code is about more than compliance—it’s about living our values. Together, we can ensure our University remains a place where respect, safety, and inclusion are non-negotiable. Reasonable Adjustments If you require any adjustments to the recruitment process to support your application, please let us know. This information will help us understand how best to support you and ensure an equitable and inclusive recruitment process. Please be assured that anything you share will be treated respectfully and appropriately. Our goal is to provide the best environment for every candidate to demonstrate their suitable skills and experience. Please reach out to employment@newcastle.edu.au if you are experiencing any difficulties or would like to discuss reasonable adjustments. Application close date: Wednesday 11 November, 2026 at 11.59pm AEDT We are excited to be Looking Ahead with you! The University of Newcastle is an equal opportunity employer committed to equity, diversity and social inclusion. Women and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander candidates are particularly encouraged to apply. The University of Newcastle acknowledges the traditional custodians of the lands within our footprint areas: Awabakal, Darkinjung, Birpai, Worimi, Wonnarua, and Eora Nations. We also pay respect to the wisdom of our Elders past and present. #LI-CP1 Related documents PD Level B Lecturer Computing and Information Technology Teaching Focused Published on 13 Oct 2026, 2:28 AM Back to top Refer someone Powered by Privacy Policy Terms of Service"
