    "https://www.livehire.com/careers/universityofnewcastle/job/6666B/T0K2L3HQJR/widening-participation-administrative-assistant": {
      "title": "Widening Participation Administrative Assistant",
      "description": "Widening Participation Administrative Assistant - University of Newcastle | Humanforce Talent Log in Humanforce LinkedIn Facebook Google Cancel Powered by ‹ Back to all jobs Widening Participation Administrative Assistant Location Callaghan NSW 2308, Australia Work Type Full Time - Fixed Term Positions 1 Position Published At: 19 hours ago Loading Refer someone Apply Job no: HRC0027622 Category: Professional, HEW 4 Join a collaborative team supporting outreach events, schools and communities Help deliver programs that create pathways to university for students 17% super, generous annual, personal, life leave and health & fitness discounts The Role As the Widening Participation Administrative Assistant, you'll play an important role in supporting the delivery of outreach and pathway programs that help students explore and access higher education opportunities. You will provide high-quality administrative, data management and customer service support to ensure the effective operation of widening participation initiatives. This is an opportunity to contribute to programs that make a meaningful difference to prospective students while undertaking the administration, communication and coordination activities that underpin successful program delivery. Working as part of a collaborative team, you'll maintain accurate records, coordinate program logistics and build positive relationships with a diverse range of stakeholders. In this role you will: Provide administrative support for widening participation and student pathway programs. Respond to enquiries from students, families, schools and University stakeholders. Maintain accurate records and data across University systems and databases. Coordinate program communications, documentation and participant information. Assist with the delivery of outreach activities, workshops and events. Prepare reports and support program tracking and compliance requirements. Contribute to the improvement of administrative processes and record management practices. This 12-month fixed-term position is full time and will be located at the Callaghan Campus (Awabakal land). How will you inspire us? We're looking for a highly organised and detail-oriented administrator with strong communication skills and a customer-focused approach. You will be confident working with data and established systems, able to manage competing priorities, and committed to delivering accurate and responsive support to students, families and stakeholders. Experience using Microsoft Office applications, particularly Excel, along with strong written communication skills and the ability to tailor communications to a range of audiences, will be highly regarded. About our University The University of Newcastle stands as a global leader distinguished by a commitment to equity and excellence. Our vision is to be a world-leading University for our regions and creating a better future for our regions and the globe. Underpinned by this vision are our values of excellence, equity, engagement and sustainability. What we offer In addition to working for a university that is breaking boundaries, discovering new ways of thinking and cementing a place as a global leader, we offer a range of benefits to our employees to ensure you balance your commitments, stay healthy and work effectively. These include: The remuneration for this position is Higher Education Worker Level 4 ($77,098 to $83,806) + 17% superannuation and is commensurate with skills and experience. Additional University Holiday days, generous personal, life, and parental leave, annual leave loading, and additional purchased leave scheme. Discounts on private health insurance, gym memberships, software, travel and more. A range of support and wellbeing programs. For more information on benefits and conditions, please follow this link . Your next steps Important information before you apply: If viewing this on a job board other than the University's please click ‘Apply’ (or equivalent) to reach the University's website, where a copy of the Position Description can be accessed. Please download a copy from the Related Documents section before commencing your application. Applications for this position will only be accepted from those with Australian residency or a valid work permit. Your application must include: Your resume. A cover letter outlining your motivation to apply for this opportunity. A statement addressing each of the essential criteria listed on the position description. Please upload your Cover Letter and response to the requested Criteria as one PDF document . This document should not exceed 3 pages. Please note: This position is known internally as the Widening Participation Data Administrator . We value diversity and strive to ensure that our recruitment processes are equitable, inclusive, and barrier-free. If you require an alternative pathway to view the Position Description or to submit an application, please reach out to employment@newcastle.edu.au . We also seek to provide a fair chance to all applicants by providing reasonable adjustments where required during the assessment and interview phases of the recruitment process. Our assessment process Typically candidates that progress beyond the shortlist stage can expect to undertake the following (minimum) assessment activities as part of the recruitment process: Interview (an ‘in person’ interview will be conducted as part of the process) Referee checks Pre-employment checks as appropriate to the role Our University is committed to creating a safe, respectful, and inclusive environment for every member of our community . This commitment is central to our values and our efforts to build a diverse workforce where everyone can thrive. As part of this, and in alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-Based Violence 2025 , from 1 January 2026, the University will also consider gender-based violence risk factors as part of our recruitment processes. The completion and return of the University’s Gender-based Violence Declaration Form will form part of the pre-employment checks for this position and any offer of employment will be conditional upon review by the University. Our adherence to the Code is about more than compliance – it’s about living our values. Together, we can ensure our University remains a place where respect, safety, and inclusion are non-negotiable. Contact Us For specific position enquiries, please contact Selina Darney, Manager Widening Participation Pathways on selina.darney@newcastle.edu.au Having technical difficulties? Please reach out to us: employment@newcastle.edu.au Please note, we do not accept email applications. Closing date: Monday, 23 November 2026 at 11.59pm AEDT We are excited to be Looking Ahead with you We provide opportunities for all people regardless of their background and experience, and this philosophy is reflected across all that we do. The University of Newcastle is an equal opportunity employer committed to equity, diversity, and social inclusion. Women and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander candidates are particularly encouraged to apply. #LI-CJ1 Related documents Position Description - Widening Participation Administrative Assistant Published on 29 Oct 2026, 5:44 AM Back to top Refer someone Powered by Privacy Policy Terms of Service"
    },
    "https://www.livehire.com/careers/universityofnewcastle/job/G44BN/4WH24C32SM/thurru-indigenous-health-unit-coordinator": {
      "title": "Thurru Indigenous Health Unit Coordinator",
      "description": "Thurru Indigenous Health Unit Coordinator - University of Newcastle | Humanforce Talent Log in Humanforce LinkedIn Facebook Google Cancel Powered by ‹ Back to all jobs Thurru Indigenous Health Unit Coordinator Location Callaghan NSW 2308, Australia Work Type Full Time - Fixed Term Positions 1 Position Published At: 19 hours ago Loading Refer someone Apply Job no: HRC0028226 Category: Professional, HEW 6 Build strong community partnerships that support Indigenous health outcomes Work alongside respected health, education and community leaders 17% super, generous annual, personal, life leave and health & fitness discounts As part of the University's commitment to increasing Indigenous employment within its workforce, this role is a targeted Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander position. The University holds an exemption under Section 126 of the Anti-Discrimination Act 1977 (NSW) in relation to its targeted recruiting programs. Both Indigenous and non-Indigenous candidates can apply, however priority will be given to those who identify as Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander in their application and successfully meet the selection criteria. The Role As the Coordinator, Thurru Indigenous Health Unit, you will lead the day-to-day operations and coordination of the Unit, supporting Indigenous health education, research and community engagement activities across the College of Health, Medicine and Wellbeing. Working closely with the Director and academic staff, you will provide high-level operational and project management support, foster strong relationships with community and industry partners, and contribute to strategic initiatives that enhance Indigenous student success, research outcomes and community impact. As part of a dedicated professional team, you will play a key role in supporting engagement with Indigenous medical and health students and advancing the goals of the Unit and College The Unit continues to provide national leadership in community-responsive Indigenous health education through the development of an empowered and capable Indigenous health workforce. In this role you will: Coordinate the day-to-day operations, events, programs and key initiatives of the Thurru Indigenous Health Unit. Supervise and support the Administrative Assistant, providing day-to-day guidance and coordination of workload. Coordinate and participate in meetings, working groups, events and community engagement activities while maintaining strong stakeholder relationships across community, government, health, research and industry sectors. Coordinate and manage multiple projects and initiatives across the Unit, ensuring milestones, timelines, budgets, risks and deliverables are effectively managed and aligned with strategic priorities. Support strategic initiatives and partnerships that strengthen Indigenous health education, workforce development, student support and community engagement. Contribute to writing research and funding proposals to support strategic initiatives. This 12-month fixed-term position is available at 0.8 FTE to 1.0 FTE (4-5 days per week) and is located at the Callaghan Campus (Awabakal Land) and may require travel to other campuses and locations as required. How will you inspire us? You will have: Experience working with Indigenous organisations and communities, or other equity groups. Proven ability to mentor, supervise and support direct reports. Demonstrated experience managing competing priorities, leading teams to achieve outcomes, and applying strong organisational and time management skills. About our University The University of Newcastle stands as a global leader distinguished by a commitment to equity and excellence. Our vision is to be a world-leading University for our regions and creating a better future for our regions and the globe. Underpinned by this vision are our values of excellence, equity, engagement and sustainability. What we offer In addition to working for a university that is breaking boundaries, discovering new ways of thinking and cementing a place as a global leader, we offer a range of benefits to our employees to ensure you balance your commitments, stay healthy and work effectively. These include: Remuneration for this position is Higher Education Worker Level 6 ($97,217 to $107,268) + 17% superannuation and is commensurate with skills and experience. A supportive workplace with a strong commitment to inclusion, equity, and cultural safety. Additional University Holiday days, annual leave loading, generous personal, life and parental leave, an additional purchased leave scheme, and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander staff leave. Discounts on private health insurance, gym memberships, software, travel and more, along with a range of support and wellbeing programs. For more information on benefits and conditions, please follow this link . Your next steps Important information before you apply: If viewing this on a job board other than the University's please click ‘Apply’ (or equivalent) to reach the University's website, where a copy of the Position Description can be accessed. Please download a copy from the Related Documents section before commencing your application. Applications for this position will only be accepted from those with Australian residency or a valid work permit. Candidates who identify as Aboriginal and/or Torries Strait Islander will be required to demonstrate their heritage by way of providing Confirmation of Aboriginality in line with University procedure . Your application must include: Your resume. A cover letter outlining your motivation to apply for this opportunity. A statement addressing each of the essential criteria listed below ( these may differ slightly from the Position Description). Demonstrated experience in an administrative leadership or coordination role in a dynamic and fast paced professional environment. Strong organisational skills including the ability to organise workflows to meet competing demands accurately and on-time and adaptability to a range of workplace expectations and responsibilities. Sound written and interpersonal and communication skills as well as the ability to perform duties in a professional, respectful and culturally sensitive manner. Demonstrated experience working with Indigenous organisations and communities, or other equity groups. Please upload your Cover Letter and response to the requested Criteria as one PDF document . This document should not exceed 3 pages. We value diversity and strive to ensure that our recruitment processes are equitable, inclusive, and barrier-free. If you require an alternative pathway to view the Position Description or to submit an application, please reach out to employment@newcastle.edu.au . We also seek to provide a fair chance to all applicants by providing reasonable adjustments where required during the assessment and interview phases of the recruitment process. Our assessment process Typically candidates that progress beyond the shortlist stage can expect to undertake the following (minimum) assessment activities as part of the recruitment process: Interview (an ‘in person’ interview will be conducted as part of the process) Referee checks Pre-employment checks as appropriate to the role Our University is committed to creating a safe, respectful, and inclusive environment for every member of our community . This commitment is central to our values and our efforts to build a diverse workforce where everyone can thrive. As part of this, and in alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-Based Violence 2025 , from 1 January 2026, the University will also consider gender-based violence risk factors as part of our recruitment processes. The completion and return of the University’s Gender-based Violence Declaration Form will form part of the pre-employment checks for this position and any offer of employment will be conditional upon review by the University. Our adherence to the Code is about more than compliance – it’s about living our values. Together, we can ensure our University remains a place where respect, safety, and inclusion are non-negotiable. Contact Us For specific position enquiries, please contact Zoe Pegler, Thurru Indigenous Health Unit Coordinator on Zoe.Pegler@newcastle.edu.au For questions regarding Aboriginality as a genuine occupational qualification for this role, or providing Confirmation of Aboriginality, please reach out to Simone Jordan, Indigenous Employment Partner on (02) 4055 0897 or via email at simone.jordan@newcastle.edu.au Having technical difficulties? Please reach out to us: employment@newcastle.edu.au Please note, we do not accept email applications. Closing date: Thursday, 12 November 2026 at 11.59pm AEDT We are excited to be Looking Ahead with you We provide opportunities for all people regardless of their background and experience, and this philosophy is reflected across all that we do. The University of Newcastle is an equal opportunity employer committed to equity, diversity, and social inclusion. Women and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander candidates are particularly encouraged to apply. #LI-CJ1 Related documents Position Description - Thurru Indigenous Health Unit Coordinator Published on 29 Oct 2026, 5:25 AM Back to top Refer someone Powered by Privacy Policy Terms of Service"
